<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Huan Chu</h1><p class="st-meta">403 CE – 404 CE · capital Jiankang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The brief usurping dynasty of Huan Xuan, who seized the Eastern Jin throne at Jiankang in 403 and was overthrown by Liu Yu within months.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-xuan">Huan Xuan</a> was the son of the great general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> and controlled Jing province on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. In 402 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, overthrowing the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-daozi">Sima Daozi's</a> family, and in 403 he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-an-of-jin">Emperor An</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> to abdicate and proclaimed himself emperor of Chu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-xuan">Huan Xuan</a> was a cultivated aristocrat and collector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, but his rule was erratic, marked by grandiose building and constant changes of policy. He lacked the loyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beifu-army">Beifu</a> army, the main northern military force.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 404 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beifu-army">Beifu</a> officers rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkou">Jingkou</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-xuan">Huan Xuan</a> fled west with the captive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-an-of-jin">Emperor An</a> and was killed. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> was restored under Liu Yu's control, the first step towards his founding of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liu-song">Liu Song</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 403 CE – 404 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.72%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>403 CE – 404 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiankang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Huan Xuan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chu (Huan Xuan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=404&amp;focus=huan-chu" aria-label="Huan Chu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7737 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Huan Chu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8057 1267L7855 1179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8057 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jiankang. <a href="/atlas?year=404&amp;focus=huan-chu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
